FILED s543q (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28, CODE 6a1301ed = MAIN-READY-114 + records, v2-16500) by the Rosetta census B (docs/rosetta-plan.md section 6b; #2104).

THE SHAPES (neutral reproducers, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    my $m = "\xff";
    my $r = ++vec($m, 0, 8);
    print "$r ", vec($m, 0, 8), "\n";
    my $q = "\x01";
    my $s = ++vec($q, 0, 1);
    print "$s ", vec($q, 0, 1), "\n";
    perl: 0 0
          0 0
    PCL : 256 0
          2 0          (exit 0, nothing on stderr)

    my $m = "\xff";
    my $r = (vec($m, 0, 8) += 1);
    print "$r ", vec($m, 0, 8), "\n";
    my $t = (vec($m, 0, 8) = 300);
    print "$t ", vec($m, 0, 8), "\n";
    my $u = "\xff"; my $w = vec($u, 0, 8)++;
    print "$w ", vec($u, 0, 8), "\n";
    perl: 0 0 / 44 44 / 255 0
    PCL : 256 0 / 300 44 / 255 0      (the postfix form is right: it answers the OLD value)

WHERE: the assignment / pre-increment of the vec place returns the value it was GIVEN; perl returns the lvalue after the
store, which for vec is the value masked to BITS bits (perl's magic: the assignment's value is the target read back).  The
same question may hold for `substr` 4-arg / lvalue substr assignment values -- not probed.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): 1053 with `do { ++vec($bitmask, $_, 8); vec($bitmask, $_, 8) } and last` (the
stored value read back) is byte-identical to perl -- its only cause.  With the bug the 9-element power set's two-byte counter
never carries out of byte 0, the `redo` never ends: the program's TIMEOUT (sizes 4 and 6 -- one byte -- are right).

PROGRAMS: 1053-Power-set (class TIMEOUT: the endless loop).
